Sūtrasthāna. (209)

The best among mudga (green gram) and śamī-grains; among waters collected from the sky (rainwater), rock salt among salts; jīvantī among pot-herbs and greens; the flesh of the eṇeya deer among game meats; the lāva bird among fowl; the monitor lizard among burrowing creatures; rohita fish among fish; clarified butter from cows among fats; cow's milk among milks; sesame oil among oils derived from plants; the fat of wild boar among the fats of anūpa (marshy-land) animals; the fat of the culukī fish among fish fats; the fat of the haṃsa (swan) among the fats of water-birds; the fat of the domestic fowl (kukkuṭa) among the fats of viṣkira birds; the fat of the wild partridge among the fats of śakuni birds; the fat of the goat among the fats derived from the flanks; fresh ginger among bulbous roots; dried grapes (raisins) among fruits; and sugar among the products derived from sugarcane — thus, the substances that are most beneficial among the dietary preparations have been described according to their primary importance. We shall now also describe those that are most harmful. Barley (yavaka) is the most unwholesome among śūka (husk) grains. Māṣa (black gram) is the most unwholesome among śamī grains; water from low-lying saline land is most unwholesome among waters; mustard greens are most unwholesome among greens; beef among the meats of domesticated animals; the young pigeon (drālaka) among birds; the frog among burrowing creatures; the cilichimo fish among fish; the fat of sheep among fats; the fat of sheep's milk among milks; the oil of safflower among plant-derived oils;

The fat of the iguana among the fats of anūpa animals; the fat of the crocodile among fish fats; the fat of the crow and the grebe among the fats of water-birds; the radish among bulbous roots; the fat of the sparrow among the fats of viṣkira birds; the fat of the elephant among the fats derived from the flanks; the likocha fruit among fruits; and the preparation mixed with phāṇita (molasses) among sugar preparations — thus, the substances that are most harmful among dietary preparations have been described according to their lowest standing. Concerning the beneficial and harmful components of dietary preparations — we shall now proceed to describe, in greater detail and with their associated qualities, the drugs according to their principal importance. As follows: water is the best among those that relieve thirst; surā (fermented drink) among those that relieve breathlessness; milk among those that are life-sustaining (jīvanīya); meat among those that are nourishing (bṛṃhaṇīya); meat-broth (rasa) among those that are refreshing (tarpaṇīya); salt among those that stimulate appetite; the domestic fowl (kukkuṭa) among those that strengthen; the hare among those that are aphrodisiac; honey among those that alleviate phlegm and bile; ghee among those that alleviate wind and bile; sesame oil among those that alleviate wind and phlegm; emesis (vamana) among those that remove phlegm; purgation (virecana) among those that remove bile; enema (basti) among those that remove wind; sudation (sveda) among those that soften; exercise (vyāyāma) among those that promote firmness; alkali (kṣāra) among those that destroy [lesions]; the tinduka (Diospyros) [is listed here as most harmful] among those that diminish appetite for food...

